popped in order: 1,2,3,5,8,9
by priority: fix outage -> write tests -> refactor
largest first: 8
```

**Approach**

Floyd's tortoise and hare: two pointers at different speeds. If they ever meet, there is a cycle. A second phase then finds where the cycle begins.

**Logic**

Why they must meet: once both are inside the loop, the fast pointer gains one position per step on the slow one, so the gap closes by exactly one each time and cannot be skipped over.

The entry-point phase rests on the distance identity — from the meeting point and from the head, advancing one step at a time converges exactly at the loop's start. That is worth deriving rather than memorising.

O(n) time, O(1) space, against O(n) memory for the hash-set approach.

**Program**

```csharp
using System;

public class ListNode
{
    public int val;
    public ListNode next;
    public ListNode(int v) { val = v; }
}

public class Solution
{
    public static bool HasCycle(ListNode head)
    {
        ListNode slow = head, fast = head;

        while (fast != null && fast.next != null)
        {
            slow = slow.next;
            fast = fast.next.next;   // gains exactly one place per step

            if (slow == fast) return true;
        }

        return false;
    }

    public static ListNode CycleStart(ListNode head)
    {
        ListNode slow = head, fast = head;

        while (fast != null && fast.next != null)
        {
            slow = slow.next;
            fast = fast.next.next;

            if (slow != fast) continue;

            // Phase two: from the head and the meeting point, one step each.
            var walker = head;
            while (walker != slow) { walker = walker.next; slow = slow.next; }

            return walker;
        }

        return null;
    }

    public static int CycleLength(ListNode head)
    {
        var start = CycleStart(head);
        if (start == null) return 0;

        int length = 1;
        for (var n = start.next; n != start; n = n.next) length++;
        return length;
    }

    private static ListNode Build(int count, int loopBackTo)
    {
        if (count == 0) return null;

        var nodes = new ListNode[count];
        for (int i = 0; i < count; i++) nodes[i] = new ListNode(i);
        for (int i = 0; i < count - 1; i++) nodes[i].next = nodes[i + 1];

        if (loopBackTo >= 0) nodes[count - 1].next = nodes[loopBackTo];
        return nodes[0];
    }

    public static void Main()
    {
        var noCycle = Build(5, -1);
        Console.WriteLine($"no cycle: {HasCycle(noCycle)}, start {CycleStart(noCycle) == null}");

        var loopsToThird = Build(5, 2);
        Console.WriteLine($"loops to index 2: {HasCycle(loopsToThird)}, " +
                          $"start {CycleStart(loopsToThird).val}, length {CycleLength(loopsToThird)}");

        var wholeList = Build(4, 0);
        Console.WriteLine($"whole list is the loop: start {CycleStart(wholeList).val}, " +
                          $"length {CycleLength(wholeList)}");

        var selfLoop = Build(1, 0);
        Console.WriteLine($"single self-loop: {HasCycle(selfLoop)}, length {CycleLength(selfLoop)}");

        Console.WriteLine($"empty: {HasCycle(null)}");
        Console.WriteLine($"single node, no loop: {HasCycle(Build(1, -1))}");
    }
}
```

**Output**

```
no cycle: False, start True
loops to index 2: True, start 2, length 3
whole list is the loop: start 0, length 4
single self-loop: True, length 1
empty: False
single node, no loop: False
```

**Approach**

O(1) average for lookup, insert and remove; O(n) worst case when hashing degenerates. The program demonstrates the degenerate case with a deliberately terrible `GetHashCode`, counting the equality comparisons each lookup performs.

**Logic**

The average case assumes the hash spreads keys evenly. When every key hashes identically, every entry lands in one bucket and lookups become a linear scan — the structure silently becomes a list.

Insertion is *amortised* O(1) rather than worst-case, because the table periodically resizes and rehashes every entry.

Enumeration order is unspecified and must never be relied on. It is not random, which is worse — it is stable enough to look dependable until an insertion pattern changes it.

**Program**

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Linq;

public class Solution
{
    private class Key
    {
        public readonly int Value;
        public static int Comparisons;
        private readonly bool _terribleHash;

        public Key(int value, bool terribleHash) { Value = value; _terribleHash = terribleHash; }

        // Legal but useless: every key lands in the same bucket.
        public override int GetHashCode() => _terribleHash ? 1 : Value.GetHashCode();

        public override bool Equals(object obj)
        {
            Comparisons++;
            return obj is Key other && other.Value == Value;
        }
    }

    private static int ProbeCost(bool terribleHash)
    {
        var set = new HashSet<Key>();
        for (int i = 0; i < 500; i++) set.Add(new Key(i, terribleHash));

        Key.Comparisons = 0;
        // Entries are prepended to the bucket chain, so the FIRST added key is last in it.
        set.Contains(new Key(0, terribleHash));
        return Key.Comparisons;
    }

    public static void Main()
    {
        Console.WriteLine($"good hash, 500 keys - comparisons for one lookup: {ProbeCost(false)}");
        Console.WriteLine($"all keys hash alike  - comparisons for one lookup: {ProbeCost(true)}");

        Console.WriteLine();
        Console.WriteLine("Lookup / Add / Remove / ContainsKey : O(1) average, O(n) worst case");
        Console.WriteLine("Add                                 : amortised, resizing rehashes all");
        Console.WriteLine("ContainsValue                       : O(n) - no index on values");
        Console.WriteLine("Count                               : O(1) - maintained, not counted");

        var dictionary = new Dictionary<string, int> { ["a"] = 1, ["b"] = 2, ["c"] = 3 };

        Console.WriteLine();
        Console.WriteLine($"ContainsKey(\"b\"):   {dictionary.ContainsKey("b")}   (hashed)");
        Console.WriteLine($"ContainsValue(2):   {dictionary.ContainsValue(2)}   (linear scan)");

        // TryGetValue does one lookup where ContainsKey plus the indexer does two.
        Console.WriteLine($"TryGetValue: {dictionary.TryGetValue("c", out int value)}, value {value}");
    }
}
```

