**Approach**

Both repeat work; recursion does it by calling itself and using the call stack for state, iteration by looping with explicit variables. The program shows the same algorithm both ways and demonstrates the stack limit that recursion imposes.

**Logic**

Recursion's cost is a stack frame per call, so depth is bounded by the stack — around ten to twenty thousand frames in .NET by default, and a StackOverflowException cannot be caught, so the process dies.

Iteration has no such limit, but for tree and graph work you often end up managing an explicit stack, which is the same structure made visible.

C# does not guarantee tail-call optimisation, so a tail-recursive function is not automatically converted to a loop as it would be in F# or Scheme.

**Program**

```csharp
using System;
using System.Runtime.CompilerServices;

public class Solution
{
    private static long FactorialRecursive(int n) =>
        n <= 1 ? 1 : n * FactorialRecursive(n - 1);

    private static long FactorialIterative(int n)
    {
        long result = 1;
        for (int i = 2; i <= n; i++) result *= i;
        return result;
    }

    // Probe the stack guard rather than crashing into it: a real StackOverflowException
    // cannot be caught and would kill the process, which is the point being made.
    private static bool RecursionHitsAStackLimit()
    {
        int depth = 0;

        void Descend()
        {
            RuntimeHelpers.EnsureSufficientExecutionStack();   // throws before the stack dies
            depth++;
            if (depth < 1000000) Descend();
        }

        try { Descend(); return false; }
        catch (InsufficientExecutionStackException) { return true; }
    }

    private static long SumIterative(int n)
    {
        long total = 0;
        for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++) total += i;   // no depth limit at all
        return total;
    }

    public static void Main()
    {
        foreach (int n in new[] { 0, 1, 5, 20 })
            Console.WriteLine($"{n}! recursive {FactorialRecursive(n)}, " +
                              $"iterative {FactorialIterative(n)}, " +
                              $"agree {FactorialRecursive(n) == FactorialIterative(n)}");

        Console.WriteLine();
        Console.WriteLine($"recursion ran out of stack before 1,000,000 frames: " +
                          $"{RecursionHitsAStackLimit()}");
        Console.WriteLine($"iteration summed to 10,000,000 with no depth at all: " +
                          $"{SumIterative(10000000)}");

        Console.WriteLine();
        Console.WriteLine("recursion: state on the call stack, matches recursive definitions");
        Console.WriteLine("           depth limited, a frame per call, no tail-call guarantee in C#");
        Console.WriteLine("iteration: explicit state, no depth limit, often less readable for trees");
    }
}
```

