**Approach**

Amortised analysis averages the cost over a *sequence* of operations, so an occasional expensive one is paid for by the many cheap ones around it. It is a worst-case guarantee over the sequence, not a probabilistic average.

**Logic**

The canonical example is a dynamic array. Appending is usually O(1), but when capacity is reached the array doubles and copies everything — O(n) for that one call. Because the capacity doubles, those copies happen at 1, 2, 4, 8 … and the total copying across n appends is under 2n, so the average is constant.

The doubling is what makes it work. Growing by a fixed amount instead gives O(n) amortised per append, which is quadratic overall — a real and common performance bug.

**Program**

```csharp
using System;

public class Solution
{
    private class GrowableArray
    {
        private int[] _items;
        private readonly bool _double;
        public int Count { get; private set; }
        public long ElementsCopied { get; private set; }
        public int Resizes { get; private set; }

        public GrowableArray(bool doubleCapacity)
        {
            _items = new int[1];
            _double = doubleCapacity;
        }

        public void Add(int value)
        {
            if (Count == _items.Length)
            {
                // Doubling: costs are rare and the total stays linear.
                // Fixed growth: resizes every few adds, and the total is quadratic.
                int capacity = _double ? _items.Length * 2 : _items.Length + 4;
                var bigger = new int[capacity];

                Array.Copy(_items, bigger, Count);
                ElementsCopied += Count;
                Resizes++;
                _items = bigger;
            }

            _items[Count++] = value;
        }
    }

    public static void Main()
    {
        Console.WriteLine($"{"appends",10}{"doubling copies",18}{"per append",13}" +
                          $"{"fixed-growth copies",22}");

        foreach (int n in new[] { 100, 1000, 10000 })
        {
            var doubling = new GrowableArray(true);
            var fixedGrowth = new GrowableArray(false);

            for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) { doubling.Add(i); fixedGrowth.Add(i); }

            Console.WriteLine($"{n,10}{doubling.ElementsCopied,18}" +
                              $"{doubling.ElementsCopied / (double)n,13:F2}" +
                              $"{fixedGrowth.ElementsCopied,22}");
        }

        var trace = new GrowableArray(true);
        for (int i = 0; i < 1000; i++) trace.Add(i);

        Console.WriteLine();
        Console.WriteLine($"1000 appends caused {trace.Resizes} resizes, " +
                          $"{trace.ElementsCopied} elements copied in total");
        Console.WriteLine("amortised O(1): the total is bounded by 2n, however it is distributed");
        Console.WriteLine("it is a worst-case guarantee over the sequence, not an average case");
    }
}
```

