**Approach**

They differ only in the comparison. A min-heap keeps the smallest at the root, a max-heap the largest — and in .NET you get the second by negating the priority rather than writing a second class.

**Logic**

The structure, the sift operations and all the complexity bounds are identical; flipping one comparator converts either into the other.

What matters in practice is picking the *opposite* of what the question seems to ask when maintaining a bounded set: to keep the k largest items you use a min-heap, because the root is then the weakest of the kept set and the right one to evict.

Negating an int priority has one trap — `int.MinValue` has no positive counterpart, so negating it overflows back to itself.

**Program**

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Linq;

public class Solution
{
    public static void Main()
    {
        var values = new[] { 5, 1, 9, 3, 7 };

        var min = new PriorityQueue<int, int>();
        var max = new PriorityQueue<int, int>();

        foreach (int v in values) { min.Enqueue(v, v); max.Enqueue(v, -v); }

        var ascending = new List<int>();
        while (min.Count > 0) ascending.Add(min.Dequeue());

        var descending = new List<int>();
        while (max.Count > 0) descending.Add(max.Dequeue());

        Console.WriteLine($"min-heap drains: {string.Join(",", ascending)}");
        Console.WriteLine($"max-heap drains: {string.Join(",", descending)}");

        // To keep the k LARGEST, use a MIN-heap: the root is the one to evict.
        const int k = 3;
        var keepLargest = new PriorityQueue<int, int>();

        foreach (int v in new[] { 4, 1, 8, 2, 9, 6 })
        {
            keepLargest.Enqueue(v, v);
            if (keepLargest.Count > k) keepLargest.Dequeue();
        }

        var topThree = new List<int>();
        while (keepLargest.Count > 0) topThree.Add(keepLargest.Dequeue());

        Console.WriteLine($"three largest (via a min-heap): {string.Join(",", topThree)}");

        // The negation trap.
        Console.WriteLine();
        Console.WriteLine($"-int.MinValue == int.MinValue: {unchecked(-int.MinValue) == int.MinValue}");
        Console.WriteLine("so negating the priority is unsafe when int.MinValue can occur;");
        Console.WriteLine("pass a reversed Comparer instead of negating.");

        var safeMax = new PriorityQueue<int, int>(Comparer<int>.Create((a, b) => b.CompareTo(a)));
        foreach (int v in new[] { int.MinValue, 0, int.MaxValue }) safeMax.Enqueue(v, v);
        Console.WriteLine($"reversed comparer, largest first: {safeMax.Dequeue()}");
    }
}
```

**Output**

```
min-heap drains: 1,3,5,7,9
max-heap drains: 9,7,5,3,1
three largest (via a min-heap): 6,8,9

-int.MinValue == int.MinValue: True
so negating the priority is unsafe when int.MinValue can occur;
pass a reversed Comparer instead of negating.
reversed comparer, largest first: 2147483647
```

**Approach**

Time counts operations as the input grows; space counts the memory held at once. They are independent axes, and algorithms routinely trade one for the other.

**Logic**

The distinction most people miss is *auxiliary* space versus total space. The input itself is usually excluded, so an in-place sort is described as O(log n) space even though it holds an O(n) array — because it allocated nothing proportional to n.

Recursion consumes space that is easy to overlook: each frame is memory, so a recursion of depth n is O(n) space even if the body allocates nothing.

The program computes the same answer three ways, counting operations and peak memory for each, so the trade is visible.

**Program**

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;

public class Solution
{
    private static long _operations;

    // O(n^2) time, O(1) auxiliary space.
    private static bool HasPairSlow(int[] values, int target)
    {
        _operations = 0;

        for (int i = 0; i < values.Length; i++)
            for (int j = i + 1; j < values.Length; j++)
            {
                _operations++;
                if (values[i] + values[j] == target) return true;
            }

        return false;
    }

    // O(n) time, O(n) auxiliary space: memory bought the speed.
    private static bool HasPairFast(int[] values, int target, out int stored)
    {
        _operations = 0;
        var seen = new HashSet<int>();

        foreach (int v in values)
        {
            _operations++;
            if (seen.Contains(target - v)) { stored = seen.Count; return true; }
            seen.Add(v);
        }

        stored = seen.Count;
        return false;
    }

    // Recursion: no allocation in the body, but O(depth) stack space.
    private static int MaxDepth;
    private static long SumRecursive(int[] values, int at, int depth)
    {
        MaxDepth = Math.Max(MaxDepth, depth);
        return at == values.Length ? 0 : values[at] + SumRecursive(values, at + 1, depth + 1);
    }

    public static void Main()
    {
        var values = new int[400];
        for (int i = 0; i < values.Length; i++) values[i] = i;

        int target = 797;   // the last possible pair, so both scan to the end

        HasPairSlow(values, target);
        long slowOperations = _operations;

        HasPairFast(values, target, out int stored);
        long fastOperations = _operations;

        Console.WriteLine($"{"approach",12}{"operations",14}{"extra memory",16}");
        Console.WriteLine($"{"nested loop",12}{slowOperations,14}{"0 items",16}");
        Console.WriteLine($"{"hash set",12}{fastOperations,14}{stored + " items",16}");

        MaxDepth = 0;
        long sum = SumRecursive(values, 0, 1);

        Console.WriteLine();
        Console.WriteLine($"recursive sum = {sum}, stack depth reached {MaxDepth}");
        Console.WriteLine("the body allocates nothing, yet the space is O(n) in frames");

        Console.WriteLine();
        Console.WriteLine("time:  how operations grow with input size");
        Console.WriteLine("space: how memory held at once grows - usually AUXILIARY, input excluded");
        Console.WriteLine("recursion costs space even when the body allocates nothing");
    }
}
```

